## Common mistakes

1. **Trusting "the agent issues refunds" without reading the preconditions.** The useful details are the ones in the limitations section: unfulfilled only, one item at a time, no 3PL sync.
2. **Skipping a confirmation step.** Intercom's own docs state that its Shopify cancel, update and refund templates ship without one. If you add none, the agent acts on the first request it parses.
3. **Letting order numbers stand in for identity.** An order number is not a credential. Prefer an agent that verifies the customer first, as Intercom's templates are documented to do.
4. **Ignoring the fulfillment lag.** A cancellation in Shopify does not stop a package already sent to a 3PL. Add a time window condition or a 3PL cancel step.
5. **Treating an MCP server as a shopper product.** AfterShip's and Loop's MCP servers are for authenticated staff, bounded by each user's own role.
6. **Forgetting the downstream dispute.** A customer who gets a refund can still file a chargeback if the refund is late. Pair a refund agent with dispute alerts.
7. **Comparing per-outcome and per-ticket prices directly.** Each vendor defines the billable unit differently. Count what you expect to be billed for, not what is quoted.

## Buying and setup checklist

- List your ten most common post-purchase requests, and mark which need a write action (cancel, refund, address) and which only need a read (status).
- For each write action, record the precondition: unfulfilled only, within a time window, under a refund cap.
- Ask every vendor for a live demo of your exact top three actions on a test store, not a recorded video.
- Confirm where the agent hands over, and that the human sees the full context.
- Check how identity is verified before the agent looks up or changes an order.
- Decide your refund cap and your auto-approve threshold, and write them down before configuring the agent.
- Log every agent action against the order, for example with an order note, so disputes can be answered.
- Test connectors in a real conversation. Intercom says its preview mode does not make live connector calls.
- Add a dispute step: alerts for pre-dispute notices and a response process for formal chargebacks.
- Set rate and scope limits on any MCP connection: Loop publishes 600 tool calls per hour per user, and both vendors tie access to the signed-in user's role.

## How we evaluated

This is a documentation review, not a lab test. On November 25, 2026 we read the pricing pages and docs for each product: Gorgias (AI Agent page, pricing page and the Shopify Actions help article), Intercom (pricing page, Fin for Ecommerce and three data connector help articles), Narvar (the agentic post-purchase page), AfterShip (pricing page and the Post-purchase MCP docs), Loop (pricing page and MCP docs), Zendesk (pricing page and the AI agent actions help article), Siena, Sierra, Decagon and Chargeflow (pricing page and a competitor-comparison blog). We built the action matrix only from what those pages state.

What we did not do: we did not connect any agent to a store, issue any refund, install any MCP server, or measure any resolution rate. All performance figures on vendor pages are vendor-reported and we did not reproduce them. We found no neutral benchmark of post-purchase action accuracy for these products, so none is cited. The only independent-looking source on this exact topic, the Chargeflow comparison blog, is written by a vendor that appears in the list, so we used it only to find leads and corroborated each product claim against the product's own pages where we could. Where a source contradicted another we say so in the text: Chargeflow's blog says Fin cancellations stay with your team while Intercom's help center lists a cancel template.

No sponsorship or affiliate relationship shaped the order of this list.

## FAQ

### Which AI agent can actually issue refunds, not just draft a reply?

Intercom Fin has the most explicit documentation: Create Shopify Refund and Refund Shopify Shipping templates, plus a Calculate Shopify Refund dry run. Gorgias AI Agent refunds as part of its cancel, remove-item and replace-item actions. Narvar and Siena describe claim approval and refunds on their marketing pages, but we found no action reference to confirm them.

### Can an AI agent cancel an order or change a shipping address?

Yes, for Shopify, in two documented cases. Gorgias AI Agent has Cancel order and Edit shipping address actions that check the order is unfulfilled first. Intercom Fin has Cancel Shopify Order and Update Shopify Order templates. Neither passes the change to a 3PL, and Gorgias says so explicitly.

### What does "unfulfilled" mean for a cancellation?

In Shopify, an unfulfilled order has not been marked as shipped. Gorgias's actions check for this before cancelling. If your warehouse picks orders before Shopify marks them fulfilled, add a time window condition (Gorgias suggests `Order Date` less than 2 hours ago) or a 3PL cancel step.

### Is there an MCP server for post-purchase actions?

Yes, two documented ones for operators. AfterShip's Post-purchase MCP server is at `https://mcp.aftership.com/post-purchase` with 21 tools, 9 read and 12 write. Loop's is at `https://api.loopreturns.com/mcp`, with documented read tools for listing returns, getting a return and reading its timeline. Narvar's page mentions an MCP orchestrator, but we could not verify its design.

### Do these agents work with WooCommerce or BigCommerce?

It depends on the vendor. Intercom states Fin for Ecommerce is Shopify-only and not available for WooCommerce, Magento, BigCommerce or custom storefronts. Gorgias's documented actions are for Shopify. Intercom's general Data connector templates also cover WooCommerce and BigCommerce for non-Fin-for-Ecommerce use, per its help article. Check each vendor for your platform before a trial.

### What is the cheapest way to start?

Chargeflow Insights is free forever, Loop Checkout+ is free for eligible merchants, and Gorgias Starter is $10 per month. For a shopper-facing agent with a published per-use price, Intercom Fin at $0.99 per outcome has no seat cost when used with another helpdesk, subject to a minimum monthly commitment. Prices are as of November 25, 2026.

### How is "resolution" or "outcome" defined, and does it matter?

It matters a lot because each vendor bills a different unit. Intercom counts an outcome when a customer confirms resolution, stops asking for help after Fin responds, or Fin completes a Procedure including handoffs. Zendesk counts an automated resolution as a request resolved without escalation. Gorgias counts an automated interaction as a request fully resolved without a human agent. Gorgias also notes that a human reply within 72 hours of an AI resolution changes the billing to a helpdesk ticket only.

### Are AI agents safe to give refund access to?

They are as safe as the guardrails you add. The documented safeguards worth requiring are a precondition check (unfulfilled), a shopper confirmation, a verified identity, a handover to a human for exceptions, and a refund cap. Intercom warns that some of its Shopify write templates ship without a confirmation step. AfterShip's MCP server requires confirmation before every write and cannot undo some of them.

### Can an agent trigger a chargeback?

Indirectly. A cancellation, an address change or a refund that comes too late can leave a customer filing a dispute anyway. Chargeflow's blog, a vendor source, recommends capping AI-triggered refunds, checking for open disputes before refunding and logging every agent action with the order. These are sensible controls whoever wrote them.

### Which tools suit enterprise teams that want custom workflows?

Decagon (natural-language Agent Operating Procedures), Sierra (one agent across chat, SMS, WhatsApp, email, voice and ChatGPT with outcome-based pricing) and Zendesk AI agents (custom actions and action flows). None of the three publishes a ready-made refund action list, so expect integration work and ask for a demo of your exact flows.

### Is there an open-source option?

Not for the products in this roundup: all ten are commercial. The open parts of the stack are the Model Context Protocol itself and community skills in the [agentskills.codes catalog](https://agentskills.codes/skills/mcp-builder) that you can adapt to write your own refund or support actions.

### How current is this information?

Prices and capabilities are as of November 25, 2026. Vendors in this space change plans often, and Gorgias, Intercom and AfterShip all show annual, monthly or promotional price points on their pages, so re-check the live pages before you buy.

